<title>JAPAN: Japan says buys 7,000 tonnes of rice.</title>
<headline>Japan says buys 7,000 tonnes of rice.</headline>
<dateline>TOKYO 1996-12-25</dateline>
<text>
<p>The Japanese Food Agency said on Wednesday it bought 7,000 tonnes of rice at an import tender.</p>
<p>Of the total, 5,773 tonnes came from the United States, 408 from China, 204 from Vietnam, 190 from Thailand, 153 from Uruguay, 153 from Egypt and 68 from Australia, the agency said.</p>
<p>It also said Japan bought 17 tonnes each from Italy, India and Indonesia.</p>
<p>The SBS tender will be the fourth such tender this year, after a 10,000-tonne tender in November.</p>
<p>Under a General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade deal in 1993, Japan agreed to gradually open up its rice market, pledging to buy 455,000 tonnes of foreign rice in fiscal 1996/97 ending next March. Its rice purchase so far this year has totalled 394,300 tonnes, including brown rice. -- Tokyo Commodity Desk (8103-3432-7391)</p>
